  {
    "id": "soft-tissue-lecture-v1-mr-2",
    "source_page_id": "mr-2",
    "source_title": "Wu and Hochman. Radiology 2009, Figure 2, PDF page 5.",
    "deck_name": "Saved Cards",
    "note_type_style": "saCloze++",
    "fields": {
      "Text": "What is the most likely diagnosis?<br><br>{{c1::Benign vascular lesion}}<br><br>1/3<br><img src=\"stlecture_mr_f02_a_v1.jpg\"><br><img src=\"stlecture_mr_f02_b_v1.jpg\"><br><img src=\"stlecture_mr_f02_c_v1.jpg\">",
      "Extra": "Entering vessels and phleboliths support a benign vascular lesion. The 2009 caption calls this a hemangioma; the images alone do not establish a modern vascular-anomaly subtype.<br><br>Wu and Hochman. Radiology 2009, Figure 2, PDF page 5.<br><br><img src=\"stlecture_mr_source_page_05_v1.jpg\"><br><a href=\"https://starfox1230.github.io/The-Library/apps/temporary-apps/library/2026-10-18-soft-tissue-lecture/papers/mri-systematic-approach.pdf#page=5\">Open source PDF</a>"
